# Story 1-1. Hostinger's git deploy clones the branch into the document root,
# so a .git directory ends up inside the web root. Harmless while the repo is
# public; a full source leak the moment it is made private. Deny it either way.
RedirectMatch 404 /\.git

# Same reasoning for any other dotfile that ends up served.
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Story 1-7. The migration redirect map. THE COMPLETE MAP, NOT A PARTIAL ONE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every URL in the current WordPress sitemap needs a decision here, because a
# missed one is a dead link for a visitor AND lost ranking for a page that
# earned it. Twelve URLs, and the eight not listed below are "keep" — they exist
# as real pages after story 1-6 and need no rule.
#
# ⚠️ TARGETS ARE RELATIVE ON PURPOSE. Apache expands a relative Location against
# the requesting host, so the SAME file is correct on staging and on production.
# Writing https://keegansmovementlab.com/... here would send every staging
# redirect to production, which is both wrong and the kind of thing that only
# shows up after cutover.
#
# ⚠️ TRAILING SLASHES. astro.config sets trailingSlash:'always' and
# build.format:'directory', matching how WordPress served these URLs, so the
# targets below all end in a slash. Without that every one of these redirects
# would land on a URL that immediately redirects again — a hop that still
# "works" and quietly dilutes what the 301 was preserving.
#
# The `/?$` in each pattern accepts the URL with or without its trailing slash,
# so an old link written either way still lands. `^` anchors matter: without it
# the /group-classes rule would also catch /services/group-classes/, which is a
# KEEP and must not redirect.

# D4, the fold. Mobility coaching was absorbed into the Chiropractic & Movement
# page rather than deleted, so this is a redirect to the content's new home and
# not to a vaguely related page.
RedirectMatch 301 ^/services/mobility-coaching/?$ /services/chiropractic-treatment/

# D4, nesting normalisation. Handstand coaching was the only service living at
# the root; it now sits under /services/ with the others.
RedirectMatch 301 ^/handstand-coaching/?$ /services/handstand/

# This one already exists on the live WordPress site and nobody had logged it.
# It must keep existing: dropping a redirect that works today is a regression
# introduced by the migration, and the most invisible kind.
RedirectMatch 301 ^/group-classes/?$ /services/group-classes/

# The default WordPress sample post. Still live, still in the sitemap, thin
# default content on an indexed URL. 410 Gone rather than 301, deliberately:
# 301 means "this moved here" and there is nowhere honest to send it, while 410
# tells Google it is deliberately gone and gets it dropped from the index faster
# than a 404 would.
RedirectMatch gone ^/hello-world-2/?$

# Story 1-2. @astrojs/sitemap emits sitemap-index.xml (plus sitemap-N.xml
# shards) and gives no option to rename them. The SEO brief requires the
# canonical path /sitemap.xml — that is the URL already registered with Search
# Console, and changing it costs the migration's own monitoring.
#
# Rewrite rather than hand-roll a sitemap: the integration stays correct as
# pages are added in 1-6, where a hand-written file would silently go stale.
# Internal rewrite, not a redirect, so /sitemap.xml IS the sitemap rather than
# pointing at one.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On
  RewriteRule ^sitemap\.xml$ /sitemap-index.xml [L]
</IfModule>
